From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 12:43:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The fence, the throne room floor, the chapel windows - and a
 dead click on every window

51 -> 37 dead Build ops, and one dead Remove that was never counted and is probably the most clicked
of the lot.

THE WINDOWS EVERY HOUSE ALREADY HAD. poh_dynamic_window is the shell the templates place 116 times
per house. The round that resolved it into a 54-child multiloc turned every hole in the walls into a
window - and a multiloc shows its ACTIVE CHILD's options, and all 54 children carry op5=Remove. So
every window in every house has had a Remove that nothing answered, which is the engine's "Nothing
interesting happens" in the most ordinary thing a player clicks.

Check 56 could not see it: it sweeps the locs the SPEC places, and a shell the TEMPLATE places is not
one of those - nor are the ops, which live on the children. Group 61 adds the sweep that was missing:
every op a placed multiloc shows through its children has to be answered. Both ends are answered now
- a window on a chapel Window space is furniture and comes out, a window in any other wall is part of
the house and says so.

My own audit script had the same blind spot for a different reason: it matched the config key as
multiloc0, multiloc1 and so on when the key is literally "multiloc" repeated, so every multiloc in
the repo read as having no options at all.

WHAT YOU CAN BUILD. The garden's fencing, seven tiers from boundary stones to a marble wall; the
throne room's floor decoration; and the chapel's nine glazings - shuttered, four decorative, four
stained glass. OSRS's own numbers, including the window experience, which really is 4 and 5 for the
two high tiers and was checked twice because it looks wrong. Nothing new had to be created: soft
clay, planks, limestone, iron, oak, teak, steel, marble, nails and molten glass were all here.

Two deliberate omissions. The throne floor's other four OSRS options drop the loser into an oubliette
this build does not have and want clockwork and magic stones it does not have either. And the Hedging
hotspots beside the fencing need seven bagged hedges that are not objs in this cache - their own
round.

THREE MORE MULTI-TILE PIECES, through the doors the combat ring opened: the "show" hand-off and an
anchor, sharing the ring's rotation maths. The throne floor and the chapel windows are the first
pieces whose ART DEPENDS ON THE HOUSE YOU LIVE IN - six floor decorations and 54 windows, one set per
style, named after the same six towns poh_styles.enum read off the template walls.

Building a chapel window reglazes the whole house: %poh_window is style x 9 + choice and the nine are
exactly what the cache has per style, so the chapel is where that choice is made and all 116 windows
follow. The 54-case table is in poh_dynamic_window's own child order because that order IS the low
digit of the varp, and group 61 checks the two against each other.

AND THE ANCHOR LIVES IN ONE PLACE. The rug round put it in furnspec.json and in a constant, and a
mutation caught them drifting. Here the generator reads it out of the spec and writes the numbers
into the file, so there is one source and nothing to drift.

rs2check 0 ERROR / 0 CHECK over 1,672 scripts and 459 configs; poh_battery ALL PASS (61 groups);
poh_mutate every one of this round's eight mutations caught, each by both the in-step check and the
specific check it was written for.

